{
  "about": "How the entry readers (daodan/core/entries.py) read the syntax tree: the framework's knowledge as data, keyed by grammar of tree-sitter-language-pack (flows spec, principle 2 and point 5.9). `grammars` names the grammar of a language the snapshot reads without tree-sitter. In `queries` one query per grammar, as lines, the forms of a binding each a pattern of its own (in an alternation nested in another, tree-sitter loses matches); a string names the grammar whose query it shares, and `code` the grammar of the expressions written as text in the markup. Captures: @tag and @element, a tag and its element; @event, the attribute as written (several nodes, from the first to the last); @handler, a name that binds when it names a function of the project; @inline, a function written in place as the handler, which binds each function of the project it calls as the snapshot resolved the call; @called, what the event calls, a candidate also when it does not resolve; @code, an expression written as text, read with the `code` grammar; @name, @hint, @text and @computed, the parts of the label; @key, the literal key of a translation call. A capture starting with _ serves a predicate; @method, the method of `this` a handler names. In `calls`, by grammar, how a call labels the control it creates or binds: `text`, a query whose @text is the content of a literal; `label`, the keywords that carry a label; `positional`, whether the first literal by position labels the control a call creates; `setters`, the methods that set it later. In `decorators`, one query per grammar for the decorators and annotations of a function or method, each read from the file's tree (the snapshot keeps them only as folded text): @decorator, one decorator, and @definition, the function it decorates; @name, a decorator's name as written; @argument, beside its @name, the text of a first argument that is a text literal. In `noir`, the entry kind each protocol of OWASP Noir's endpoints is; another protocol stays a shape to classify.",
